  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Rebuilding a new and all-inclusive Haiti: strengthening Haitian civil society in rebuilding Haiti</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The powerful earthquake of 7.0 magnitudes (USGS) on the Richter scale affected Haiti, at 16.53hrs local time (GMT 21.53hrs). The earthquake happened 17km south-west of Port-au-Prince, the capital of Haiti. Aftershocks measured 5.9 and 5.5 respectively in the first hours after the quake and continue to occur. The Direction for Civil Protection (DCP) estimates that 222,517 people died, 300,600 were wounded and approximately 3 million have been affected by the earthquake, including 1.8 million who have lost their homes and are in need of shelter. Around 1,301,491 are the number of internally displaced people living in spontaneous/organized settlements and more than 600,000 have been displaced outside the earthquake affected areas. The Jesuit Refugee and Migrant Service (JRSM), one of the Progressio partners, is currently managing 7 of these IDP camps accommodating 1,189 individuals. They are facing different kind of challenges because of not only the scale of the emergency but also their experience to manage such complex situation. One the other hand some Haitian Civil Society Organisations, long time Progressio Partners, feel marginalised from decision making processes on reconstruction.
The project, Rebuilding a new and all inclusive Haiti: strengthening Haitian civil society in rebuilding their country has two components; a) monitoring the international aid for the reconstruction of Haiti (advocacy &amp; communications) and b) institutional strengthening of the JRSM (camp management).
The overall programme objective is to strengthen civil society and local government responses to the long term reconstruction and development of Haiti, especially those working on the Progressio core themes of governance/participation and environment:</narrative>
  </description>
